const protocolFillIdeas: IdeaInput[] = [
  ideaFill({ id: "idea-pu-contrast-ct-screening-questions", name: "Contrast CT booked on three kidney screening questions, without a routine blood test beforehand", maturity: "early-clinical", actor: "clinic", cost: "small", horizonYears: 2,
    sections: ["imaging"], tags: T("untested", "insufficient"),
    tldr: "Many hospitals still ask for a kidney blood test within three months before a CT scan with contrast dye, and cancel scans when it is missing. Since 2024 NICE has said a test from the past six months will do, and that when there is none, three questions about kidney disease and acute illness can decide whether a test is needed. How safely and how widely this works is not measured.",
    summary: "The recommendation. NICE NG148 (updated October 2024) says that before a non-urgent contrast CT the requester should use an eGFR from the past 6 months if there is one; if there is none, ask whether the person has kidney disease or a kidney transplant, has seen or is waiting to see a kidney specialist, kidney surgeon or urologist, or has symptoms of acute illness likely to cause acute kidney injury; and, if the answers do not suggest kidney disease and the person is clinically stable, consider proceeding \"without the need for further blood tests before the scan\" (1.1.10 to 1.1.12). The European ESUR 2026 handbook instead measures eGFR within 7 days for inpatients and the acutely ill and within 3 months for others, either in everyone or in those with a listed history.\n\nWhy it would matter. NICE's committee wrote that in current practice \"a person is required to have an eGFR test in the 3 months before undergoing contrast media CT scanning\", that this \"often results in delayed scans\", and that the change is likely to reduce eGFR testing and short-notice cancellations. People with cancer have many contrast scans, so each avoidable test and cancellation recurs.\n\nWhy it is untested. The same committee found the evidence for risk questionnaires \"lacking in both quantity and quality\" and made the recommendation on its own knowledge and expertise, with a research recommendation on validated tools. No study OnCo found measures how many trusts have adopted the questions, how many tests and cancellations they save, or how many people with unrecognised kidney impairment they miss.",
    hypothesis: "Booking non-urgent contrast CT on the three NICE screening questions, with an eGFR only when an answer is positive or no result exists from the past six months, reduces pre-scan blood tests and cancellations by at least half without an increase in contrast-associated acute kidney injury.",
    rationale: "The threshold for extra care is an eGFR below 30 in all three major guidelines, and the risk above it is low; a three-month blood-test rule therefore spends tests and scan slots on people who are almost all safe. The questions are cheap, but their sensitivity for finding the few who are not safe is unknown.",
    test: "A before-and-after or stepped-wedge study across imaging departments adopting the questions, measuring blood tests per scan, cancellations, and acute kidney injury at 48 to 72 hours in a sample of people who screened negative and had an eGFR checked after the scan.",
    technologies: ["iodinated-contrast-media", "ct"], institutions: ["nice"],
    bottlenecks: ["b-care-fragmentation", "b-knowledge-diffusion"], related: ["idea-pu-no-fasting-before-contrast"],
    links: [NICE_NG148_RECS, NICE_NG148_RATIONALE, ESUR_2026] }),

  ideaFill({ id: "idea-pu-contrast-switch-agent-after-reaction", name: "Switching to a different contrast agent, chosen by allergy testing, instead of steroid premedication after a reaction", maturity: "early-clinical", actor: "clinic", cost: "small", horizonYears: 3,
    sections: ["imaging"], tags: T("promising", "moderate"),
    tldr: "People who reacted to a contrast dye are often given steroid tablets before their next scan with the same dye. In a large study, people given a different dye had a repeat reaction in 3 per cent of scans, against 19 per cent with the same dye and steroids. European radiologists now recommend changing the dye, ideally after allergy testing, and say premedication has a narrow place.",
    summary: "The evidence. McDonald and colleagues reviewed 1,973 people with a previous allergic-like reaction who had 4,360 later contrast CT scans at one United States centre between 2009 and 2017. Repeat reactions occurred in 80 of 423 examinations (19 per cent) with the same contrast agent and steroid premedication, and in 10 of 322 (3 per cent) with a different agent and no steroids (odds ratio 0.14, 95 per cent confidence interval 0.06 to 0.33). At the first repeat scan, people given the same agent reacted at the same rate with or without steroids (26 per cent against 25 per cent). It is a retrospective single-centre cohort, not a trial.\n\nThe guideline. The ESUR Contrast Media Safety Committee (European Radiology 2025) recommends referring at least everyone with a moderate or severe reaction to a drug allergy specialist and choosing an alternative agent from the allergy evaluation, which it calls safer and more effective than premedication. It cites an American allergology guideline finding evidence lacking for corticosteroid premedication, and notes that steroids can raise blood glucose. The ACR Manual on Contrast Media, the main source of North American premedication regimens, could not be read for this record, so the North American position is named but not quoted.\n\nThe open questions. There is no randomised comparison of switching against premedication, allergy services able to test contrast agents are scarce in many places, and how often departments switch rather than premedicate has not been measured.",
    hypothesis: "After a contrast reaction, recording the culprit agent and switching to a structurally different agent (chosen by skin testing where available) halves repeat reactions compared with the same agent under steroid cover, without delaying cancer imaging.",
    rationale: "The best data available show premedication with the same agent barely changes the repeat rate, while switching cuts it several-fold; switching costs nothing once the culprit is recorded, and avoids steroid side effects in people who may be diabetic or on chemotherapy.",
    test: "A pragmatic randomised or cluster-randomised comparison, in people with a prior mild or moderate immediate reaction needing repeat contrast CT, of switching agent without premedication against the same agent with standard steroid premedication, with repeat reaction as the primary endpoint.",
    technologies: ["iodinated-contrast-media", "ct"],
    bottlenecks: ["b-toxicity-qol", "b-knowledge-diffusion"],
    links: [MCDONALD_2021, ESUR_HSR_2025, ESUR_2026] }),

  ideaFill({ id: "idea-pu-no-fasting-before-contrast", name: "Eating and drinking normally before a contrast CT scan", maturity: "being-tested-at-scale", actor: "clinic", cost: "small", horizonYears: 1,
    sections: ["imaging"], tags: T("proven-unevenly-used", "strong"),
    tldr: "Being told not to eat for hours before a CT scan with contrast dye dates from older dyes that made people sick. Randomised trials, including one of more than 3,000 outpatients at a cancer centre, found no more sickness when people ate, and European radiologists recommend against fasting. Some hospitals still ask for it.",
    summary: "The guideline. The ESUR 2026 handbook: \"Fasting is not recommended before administration of low- or iso-osmolar non-ionic iodine-based contrast media, gadolinium-based agents or ultrasound contrast agents\", explaining that the practice dates from high-osmolar agents that made many patients vomit. FDG PET is different: there, fasting is part of the test.\n\nThe trials. At a Brazilian cancer centre, 3,206 outpatients were randomised to fast for at least 4 hours or have a light meal; adverse symptoms after contrast were reported by 1.5 per cent and 0.9 per cent, not a significant difference (Barbosa 2018). In 2,091 hospital inpatients randomised to fast for at least 4 hours or eat freely, there was no aspiration pneumonitis in either group and nausea (6.6 against 7.6 per cent) and vomiting (2.6 against 3.0 per cent) did not differ significantly (Neeman 2021). In 1,103 Spanish outpatients, nausea and vomiting were less common without fasting (0.92 against 2.86 per cent) and overall acute reactions did not differ (Zitan Saidi 2024). A meta-analysis of ten studies with 308,013 patients found no aspiration pneumonia and the same pooled rate of nausea, 4.6 per cent, with and without fasting (Choi 2023).\n\nThe gap. No survey OnCo found measures how many departments still ask patients to fast; the authors of the 2024 trial write that many European patients still experience the practice. For people with cancer who are already eating poorly, or who have diabetes, hours without food before every staging scan is a burden with no benefit shown.",
    hypothesis: "Removing fasting instructions from appointment letters for routine contrast CT does not increase nausea, vomiting or aspiration, and improves patient experience and attendance, particularly among people with cancer having repeated scans.",
    rationale: "Four trials and a meta-analysis of more than 300,000 patients agree, and the European guideline is explicit; what remains is changing letters and local habits.",
    test: "An audit of appointment letters and departmental policies for contrast CT across a national network, followed by a before-and-after measure of emetic reactions, aspiration events and patient-reported experience where fasting instructions are removed.",
    technologies: ["iodinated-contrast-media", "ct", "gadolinium-based-contrast-agents"],
    bottlenecks: ["b-knowledge-diffusion", "b-toxicity-qol"], related: ["idea-pu-contrast-ct-screening-questions"],
    links: [ESUR_2026, BARBOSA_2018, NEEMAN_2021, ZITAN_SAIDI_2024, CHOI_2023] }),

  ideaFill({ id: "idea-pu-sglt2-glp1-plan-before-pet", name: "A written plan for SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 medicines before an FDG PET-CT scan", maturity: "speculative", actor: "clinic", cost: "small", horizonYears: 3,
    sections: ["imaging"], tags: T("untested", "insufficient", ["precision"]),
    tldr: "PET-CT scans for cancer measure how much sugar tissues take up, and many people now take newer diabetes and weight-loss medicines that change sugar handling. The main European guideline predates their wide use and says nothing about them, while reports suggest they can blur parts of the scan. There is no agreed advice on whether to pause them.",
    summary: "The guideline gap. The EANM FDG PET/CT procedure guideline (version 2.0, 2015), the reference for preparing adults, covers fasting, blood glucose, insulin and metformin but not sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitors or glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonists. A joint Australian Diabetes Society and Australian and New Zealand Society of Nuclear Medicine guideline for people with diabetes (summarised in 2026) covers oral glucose-lowering medicines; only its summary abstract was read, so what it advises for these classes is not reported here.\n\nWhat the reports show. In 262 women with breast cancer having FDG PET/CT, thyroid uptake was less frequent and lower in the 55 taking an SGLT2 inhibitor, independent of glucose control (Alanli 2026). In a propensity-matched oncology cohort of 187 pairs from 6,747 scans, heart uptake was absent in 64.7 per cent of those taking an SGLT2 inhibitor against 37.4 per cent of those not (Sperry 2025). For GLP-1 medicines the evidence is case reports: a same-day semaglutide injection was followed by diffuse muscle uptake that obscured lesions, which cleared on a scan after the drug had been stopped for 6 days (Hong 2026), and a second report describes muscle and heart uptake complicating interpretation (Oldan 2024). None of these studies tested a preparation rule.\n\nWhat this means. Whether to pause these medicines, for how long, and how that weighs against blood glucose on the day, is decided department by department.",
    hypothesis: "A stated preparation rule for SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists before FDG PET-CT (for example, timing of the last GLP-1 injection relative to the scan) reduces non-diagnostic or equivocal scans without unsafe blood glucose.",
    rationale: "Use of both classes has grown fast among people of the age at which cancer is staged; the signals that they alter FDG distribution are consistent across a cohort, a matched analysis and case reports, and a preparation rule costs nothing if it works.",
    test: "A prospective multicentre cohort recording drug, dose and timing against blinded image-quality scores and lesion conspicuity, followed by a randomised comparison of continuing against a timed pause for GLP-1 receptor agonists if the cohort shows an effect.",
    technologies: ["fdg-pet", "pet-ct"], institutions: ["eanm"],
    bottlenecks: ["b-knowledge-diffusion"],
    links: [EANM_FDG_2015, ADS_ANZSNM_2026, ALANLI_2026, SPERRY_2025, HONG_2026, OLDAN_2024] }),

  ideaFill({ id: "idea-pu-lmwh-plan-around-endoscopy", name: "Guidance on treatment-dose low molecular weight heparin for a cancer clot around endoscopy", maturity: "speculative", actor: "clinic", cost: "small", horizonYears: 3,
    sections: ["supportive-care"], tags: T("untested", "insufficient"),
    tldr: "Many people with cancer inject a blood thinner called low molecular weight heparin every day to treat a clot, especially those with bowel or stomach cancers, who are also the people most often needing an endoscopy. The main endoscopy guideline only covers this drug as a short bridge for people coming off warfarin, so teams have no agreed plan for when to stop and restart it.",
    summary: "The gap. The BSG and ESGE guideline on endoscopy in people on antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy (2021) covers low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) as a bridge while warfarin is stopped: start a therapeutic dose two days after stopping warfarin, give the last dose at least 24 hours before the procedure, restart it the day after. It gives no separate advice for people on long-term treatment-dose LMWH for cancer-associated thrombosis.\n\nWhy it matters for cancer. NICE NG158 (2020) recommends considering a direct oral anticoagulant for people with active cancer and a confirmed clot, and LMWH alone or with a vitamin K antagonist if a DOAC is unsuitable (1.3.17, 1.3.18). Its committee found a higher rate of gastrointestinal and genitourinary bleeding with DOACs than with LMWH in active cancer and agreed DOACs may be unsuitable \"such as people with gastrointestinal malignancies\". So the people steered towards LMWH are disproportionately those having endoscopies for diagnosis, stenting or bleeding.\n\nWhat is unknown. The timing of the last dose before high-risk procedures, whether a reduced dose should be given in the days around them, and when to restart after polypectomy or sphincterotomy are left to local practice; no study OnCo found compares approaches in this group.",
    hypothesis: "A stated periprocedural plan for therapeutic LMWH in cancer-associated thrombosis (last dose and restart timed by procedure bleeding risk and clot recency) reduces both post-procedure bleeding and early recurrent clots compared with ad hoc local practice.",
    rationale: "The drug, the population and the procedures are common together; the guideline that governs endoscopy names LMWH only as a bridge, and the guideline that governs the clot steers gastrointestinal cancers towards LMWH.",
    test: "A prospective multicentre registry of people on therapeutic LMWH for cancer-associated thrombosis undergoing endoscopy, recording timing and outcomes, to inform a consensus statement and then a pragmatic comparison of two timing rules for high-risk procedures.",
    technologies: ["cancer-associated-thrombosis"], terms: ["endoscopy"], cancers: ["colorectal", "gastric", "pancreatic"], institutions: ["nice"],
    bottlenecks: ["b-care-fragmentation", "b-toxicity-qol"],
    links: [BSG_ESGE_2021, NICE_NG158] }),

  ideaFill({ id: "idea-pu-photobiomodulation-every-head-neck-centre", name: "Photobiomodulation to prevent oral mucositis available in every head and neck radiotherapy centre", maturity: "being-tested-at-scale", actor: "clinic", cost: "medium", horizonYears: 3,
    sections: ["supportive-care", "radiation"], tags: T("proven-unevenly-used", "strong"),
    tldr: "Radiotherapy to the head and neck usually makes the mouth and throat raw and painful. Shining low-level laser light inside the mouth during treatment reduces this, and the international supportive care guideline has recommended it since 2020, with its highest level of evidence for people also having chemotherapy. In a survey of 25 Italian radiotherapy centres, one used it.",
    summary: "The guideline. The MASCC/ISOO mucositis guideline (2020) recommends intraoral photobiomodulation with low-level laser to prevent oral mucositis in adults having chemoradiotherapy for head and neck cancer (level of evidence I) and in those having radiotherapy alone (level II), using the parameters of one of the tested protocols in its Table 2. It asks clinicians to tell patients about the conflicting animal evidence on whether light can affect tumour behaviour, and to consider safety in people with oral cancer.\n\nThe measured gap. A 2023 survey of 25 Italian radiation oncology centres found that, although low-energy laser is included in published recommendations and guidelines, \"only one center involved in the present survey uses LEL in its clinical practice\"; products without supporting evidence, such as mucoadhesive agents, were used more often. No national figure for the UK or the United States was found.\n\nWhat stands in the way. Equipment, trained staff and treatment time each day of radiotherapy, and the unresolved tumour-safety question, which is why the guideline pairs the recommendation with a conversation.",
    hypothesis: "Offering guideline-protocol intraoral photobiomodulation to every adult having radical head and neck radiotherapy reduces severe oral mucositis, feeding-tube use and unplanned treatment breaks at the population level, with no detectable effect on locoregional control.",
    rationale: "The benefit has the highest guideline grade available in supportive care for one of the most painful effects of cancer treatment, and the one survey measuring use found it in one centre in twenty-five.",
    test: "A network-level implementation study with severe mucositis, feeding-tube placement and treatment interruption as outcomes, and locoregional control followed through cancer registries to address the tumour-safety question.",
    technologies: ["photobiomodulation-mucositis"], terms: ["mucositis"], cancers: ["head-and-neck", "oral-cavity-cancer", "oropharyngeal-cancer"], institutions: ["mascc"],
    bottlenecks: ["b-toxicity-qol", "b-knowledge-diffusion"], related: ["idea-moon-mucositis-taste-programme"],
    links: [MASCC_MUCOSITIS_2020, BERGAMASCHI_2023] }),

  ideaFill({ id: "idea-pu-nurse-led-pelvic-radiation-disease-clinic", name: "A nurse-led clinic for bowel problems after pelvic radiotherapy in every cancer centre", maturity: "being-tested-at-scale", actor: "clinic", cost: "medium", horizonYears: 3,
    sections: ["supportive-care", "radiation"], tags: T("proven-unevenly-used", "moderate"),
    tldr: "Bowel problems lasting months or years after radiotherapy to the pelvis are common and often treatable once their causes are found. In a randomised trial, a trained nurse following a step-by-step plan helped as much as a specialist doctor, and both helped more than a self-help booklet. Specialist services exist in some places and not others.",
    summary: "The trial. ORBIT randomised 218 people in London with new bowel symptoms persisting 6 months after pelvic radiotherapy to a detailed self-help booklet, algorithm-based care led by a gastroenterologist, or the same algorithm led by a nurse. At 6 months, bowel symptom scores (IBDQ-B) improved more with the nurse than the booklet (mean difference 4.12, 95 per cent confidence interval 0.04 to 8.19) and with the gastroenterologist than the booklet (5.47, 1.14 to 9.81), and the nurse group was not inferior to the gastroenterologist group. The authors concluded that for most patients the care can be given by a trained nurse. The algorithm is published as guidance (Andreyev 2015).\n\nIn practice. The EAGLE study set up two nurse-led and one doctor-led service in three UK cancer centres for men after prostate radiotherapy: of 339 men recruited, 91 screened positive and 58 accepted referral; diagnoses included radiation proctopathy, bile acid malabsorption, food intolerances or bacterial overgrowth, and vitamin B12 or D deficiency. Staff costs were estimated at 117 to 185 pounds per patient depending on the model, and staff worried about funding beyond the study.\n\nThe gap. No survey OnCo found counts how many centres run such a clinic. A 2025 qualitative study of 28 survivors and 19 professionals in England described geographical disparity in late-effects services and survivors feeling \"cast adrift\".",
    hypothesis: "A nurse-led pelvic radiation disease clinic in each radiotherapy centre, with routine screening for bowel symptoms at follow-up, improves bowel-specific quality of life at a population level and finds treatable causes in most people referred.",
    rationale: "One randomised trial shows the model works with nurses, an implementation study shows it can be set up in ordinary centres at modest staff cost, and qualitative work shows people are left without it.",
    test: "A stepped-wedge rollout across radiotherapy networks with routine screening, uptake, diagnoses made and change in bowel-specific quality of life as outcomes, and a national survey of service provision as the baseline.",
    cancers: ["prostate", "cervical", "endometrial", "rectal-cancer", "anal"], terms: ["late-effects"],
    bottlenecks: ["b-survivorship", "b-toxicity-qol", "b-care-fragmentation"],
    links: [ORBIT_2013, ANDREYEV_2015, EAGLE_2025, BIRAN_2025] }),

  ideaFill({ id: "idea-pu-lutetium-177-family-planning-table", name: "One aligned table of contraception and breastfeeding intervals after lutetium-177 therapy", maturity: "speculative", actor: "policy", cost: "small", horizonYears: 2,
    sections: ["radiopharma"], tags: T("untested", "insufficient"),
    tldr: "After lutetium-177 treatment for prostate cancer or neuroendocrine tumours, patients are told how long to avoid pregnancy or breastfeeding, but the numbers differ by source: 3 months, 14 weeks, 4 months, 6 months or 7 months. A single table, with the reasoning for each figure, would let teams and families give the same answer.",
    summary: "What the sources say. The US label for Pluvicto (lutetium-177 vipivotide tetraxetan) advises men with female partners of reproductive potential to use effective contraception for 14 weeks after the last dose. The US label for Lutathera (lutetium-177 dotatate) advises women of reproductive potential 7 months and men 4 months, and no breastfeeding for 2.5 months after the last dose. The joint EANM/SNMMI procedure guideline for lutetium-177 PSMA therapy (2023) advises contraception during and for 3 months after therapy, and introduces this advice with the words \"Without specific evidence for 177Lu-PSMA-RLT\". The joint IAEA, EANM and SNMMI guidance on peptide receptor radionuclide therapy (2013) advises women to avoid pregnancy for at least 6 months. The European product information could not be read by script, so European and UK figures are not included.\n\nWhy it matters. A family hears different numbers from a leaflet, a website and a clinician, with no way to know which applies or why. The figures derive from different reasoning (radiation dose, genotoxicity, sperm and egg development cycles, drug excretion) that the documents mostly do not state side by side.\n\nWhat is unknown. No study compares the intervals, and none has measured whether patients understand or follow them.",
    hypothesis: "A single table issued jointly by the nuclear medicine societies, giving for each lutetium-177 product the contraception interval for women and for men and the breastfeeding interval, with the basis of each figure, reduces conflicting advice and improves patient understanding.",
    rationale: "The disagreement is visible in documents a patient may read on the same day, and resolving it needs a consensus statement rather than new research.",
    test: "A society Delphi to produce the table, followed by a randomised comparison of patient understanding using current leaflets against a leaflet built on the aligned table.",
    drugs: ["pluvicto", "lutathera"], cancers: ["prostate", "neuroendocrine"], technologies: ["fertility-preservation"], institutions: ["eanm", "snmmi", "iaea"],
    bottlenecks: ["b-regulatory-fragmentation", "b-knowledge-diffusion"],
    links: [PLUVICTO_USPI, LUTATHERA_USPI, EANM_SNMMI_PSMA_2023, IAEA_PRRNT_2013] }),

  ideaFill({ id: "idea-pu-dental-extraction-healing-interval", name: "Settling the minimum gap between dental extraction and the start of head and neck radiotherapy", maturity: "early-clinical", actor: "research", cost: "medium", horizonYears: 5,
    sections: ["supportive-care", "radiation"], tags: T("untested", "insufficient"),
    tldr: "Teeth that might cause trouble are often taken out before radiotherapy to the jaw, to lower the risk of the bone breaking down later. How long the sockets should heal before radiotherapy starts is not settled: waiting longer may help the jaw but delays cancer treatment, and the studies so far disagree.",
    summary: "The evidence. A 2026 meta-analysis of eight retrospective cohorts (47,527 people with head and neck cancer, 2,863 cases of osteoradionecrosis) found no overall association between the extraction-to-radiotherapy interval and osteoradionecrosis (odds ratio 0.99, 95 per cent confidence interval 0.67 to 1.47), with very high heterogeneity (I² 92 per cent). In Western populations, starting radiotherapy within 7 days of extraction was associated with higher risk (odds ratio 3.07, 1.52 to 6.21), as was starting within 14 days (2.01, 1.10 to 3.68). A 2022 meta-analysis of seven studies (875 people treated with IMRT) found that extractions before IMRT were associated with less osteoradionecrosis than extractions after, but the prediction interval included no effect and the authors concluded the benefit was not proven, to be balanced against delay to cancer treatment.\n\nThe guideline. The ISOO-MASCC-ASCO guideline on osteoradionecrosis (2024) is the current international standard; only its abstract could be read for this record, so whether it states an interval is not reported here.\n\nWhat is unknown. Every study is retrospective and none randomises the interval; how much delay to radiotherapy is acceptable for a given tumour is a separate question the dental studies do not address.",
    hypothesis: "A minimum healing interval of about two weeks between extraction and radiotherapy lowers osteoradionecrosis without worsening tumour control, compared with starting radiotherapy as soon as planning allows.",
    rationale: "The pooled subgroup signal in Western cohorts points at the first one to two weeks, the overall analysis does not, and the decision is made for thousands of people a year with delay to cancer treatment on the other side of the scale.",
    test: "A prospective multicentre cohort with interval recorded to the day, dental dose and osteoradionecrosis at two years, nested in pathways that already time planning around dental clearance; a randomised comparison of a short and a longer interval if equipoise is confirmed.",
    cancers: ["head-and-neck", "oral-cavity-cancer", "oropharyngeal-cancer"], terms: ["late-effects"], institutions: ["mascc", "asco"],
    bottlenecks: ["b-toxicity-qol", "b-survivorship"],
    links: [LIU_2026, BALERMPAS_2022, ISOO_MASCC_ASCO_ORN_2024] }),

  ideaFill({ id: "idea-pu-pregnancy-test-or-ask-before-radiotherapy", name: "Routine pregnancy testing compared with asking, before radiotherapy", maturity: "speculative", actor: "clinic", cost: "small", horizonYears: 3,
    sections: ["radiation"], tags: T("untested", "insufficient"),
    tldr: "Before radiotherapy, people who could be pregnant are asked whether they might be; some departments also do a pregnancy test for everyone in an age range. Which approach misses fewer pregnancies, and at what cost in delays and false alarms, has never been compared. Some cancers make the pregnancy hormone themselves and can give a false positive test.",
    summary: "The rules. In the UK, the Royal College of Radiologists' IR(ME)R guidance for radiotherapy (second edition, 2026) leaves the age range for enquiries and any routine testing to each employer's procedure: it allows routine testing for people with irregular cycles after a risk assessment, says a test should be done if pregnancy cannot be excluded, and, for molecular radiotherapy, that menstrual history alone is not adequate to exclude pregnancy.\n\nWhat practice looks like. In an international survey of 434 radiation oncologists (69.1 per cent in the United States), 84.8 per cent agreed one should screen for pregnancy before radiotherapy, yet 29.7 per cent said their department had no screening policy and 7.1 per cent did not screen. Of 93 who had treated a known pregnant patient, 17 had done so accidentally, and not screening at simulation was associated with treating a pregnant patient (p = 0.0459).\n\nThe catch with testing. Some tumours secrete human chorionic gonadotropin and give a positive pregnancy test in people who are not pregnant; one report describes a gastrointestinal stromal tumour found this way before radiotherapy (Andruska 2020).\n\nWhat is unknown. No study compares asking alone with routine testing for missed pregnancies, delays or false positives in radiotherapy.",
    hypothesis: "Routine urine pregnancy testing at the planning scan for everyone of reproductive potential, on top of a structured enquiry, finds pregnancies that enquiry misses at an acceptable rate of false positives and delays.",
    rationale: "A missed pregnancy in radiotherapy is rare but grave, policies vary widely, the only practice survey links not screening at simulation with accidental treatment, and the cost of a urine test is small; but tumour-made hormone can mislead, so testing has its own harms.",
    test: "A multicentre audit linking enquiry and test results at planning to later-recognised pregnancies, followed if justified by a cluster comparison of structured enquiry alone against enquiry plus routine testing.",
    terms: ["cancer-in-pregnancy"],
    bottlenecks: ["b-knowledge-diffusion"],
    links: [RCR_IRMER_2026, ZAKI_2021, ANDRUSKA_2020] }),
];

